<p>This doesn't do justice to what is an interesting core idea (protein folding = origami, what would a protein do if it could wish?).</p>
<ul>
<li>The pseudo-science doesn't make sense. If only one misfolded protein is produced every 2 weeks, how would this ever get to 5g in mass, let alone 78g? Based on my rough calculations (happy to be proven wrong) it would take far longer than the total lifespan of the universe.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The choice to put Addendum 1 (which is not consistently capitalised) in the discussion page is odd. It breaks immersion (especially as the Discussion page post in question starts with an out-of-universe author note) and doesn't appear particularly necessary. Do you need Addendum 1 at all?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>You spend a footnote justifying how the proteins can see presented items, without the Foundation seeming to be particularly interested in how these proteins are significantly smarter than domestic animals. The jump to recognising and responding to emotional indicators was particularly big. Also, how does the protein know which items are &quot;presented to it&quot; - ie why is it not always folding to imitate the shape of petri dishes, or substrate molecules?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The body horror of the last three paragraphs of the Description seems completely out of place tonally, never pays off and requires a host of new &quot;powers&quot; for the proteins, like drilling out of cells. Why don't the proteins recognise the emotional reactions of people they're mutilating?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Perhaps it's just me, but I didn't understand the significance of Mr Adachi at all. The medical status updates seem particularly odd - did the proteins use their wish to ask for <em>Strep. pneumoniae</em> as playmates?</li>
</ul>
<p>I think this would be significantly improved by going back to the drawing board and concentrating on things that better tell the story of what a sentient protein might do, if it existed.</p>

